  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "**This is the easy version of the problem. Here you need to construct any valid permutation PPP. In the hard version, you need to count the number of valid permutations.**\n**This version is worth 100100100 points Division 111 and unscoreable in other Divisions.**\n\nStack had a permutation PPP of the integers [0,N−1][0, N - 1][0,N−1].\n\nBased on that, he calculated an array AAA of length NNN as follows:\n\n\nAi=mex(P1,P2,...,Pi)A_i = \\text{mex}(P_1, P_2, ..., P_i)Ai​=mex(P1​,P2​,...,Pi​) **OR** Ai=mex(Pi,Pi+1,...,PN)A_i = \\text{mex}(P_i, P_{i + 1}, ..., P_N)Ai​=mex(Pi​,Pi+1​,...,PN​)\nNow, Stack forgot the original permutation PPP.\n\nIn this version, he needs to **find any valid permutation** PPP that could have been the original permutation, given the array AAA. It may be possible that none exist, in which case print −1-1−1.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of multiple lines of input.\n\nThe first line contains NNN - the size of the permutation.\nThe second line contains NNN elements - A1,A2,...,ANA_1, A_2, ..., A_NA1​,A2​,...,AN​.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the following:\n\n\n\nif a valid permutation exists, NNN integers - P1,P2,....,PNP_1, P_2, ...., P_NP1​,P2​,....,PN​. (0≤Pi&lt;N,Pi≠Pj0 \\le P_i &lt; N, P_i \\ne P_j0≤Pi​&lt;N,Pi​=Pj​ for i≠ji \\ne ji=j)\n−1-1−1 otherwise.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\le T \\le 10^51≤T≤105",
      "1≤N≤2⋅1051 \\le N \\le 2 \\cdot 10^51≤N≤2⋅105",
      "0≤Ai≤N0 \\le A_i \\le N0≤Ai​≤N",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceed 2⋅1052 \\cdot 10^52⋅105"
    ],
